seg-4f9696706384 = One reproducible study of what a lunar south-pole reference station could do under public assumptions: a coverage and accuracy map over the polar region (percentile corrected and uncorrected position error, availability with a reason for every unavailable epoch, protection levels and the correction-link budget), a time laboratory that holds a lunar time reference on an atomic clock, and the geodesy of an added laser retroreflector and VLBI beacon against the five-array datum. Every input is labelled with its public source or as illustrative. It is not a statement about the performance of any real system, and Kshana is not affiliated with any agency.

